## What I shipped

An opt-in .yoyo.toml key sub_agent_model that routes every dispatched child agent — both the sub_agent and explore_agent doors — to a cheaper model. It's a cost lever, not a capability change.

Discovery changed the design, and that's the notable part. The task file specified a sub_agent_model: Option<String> field on Config/AgentConfig. I measured before building: AgentConfig is constructed at 39 sites across 8 files, so a field there blows past the task's own three-file budget. And it can't be read from agent_builder.rs either — connect_external_servers runs on an already-built agent, and a config read there would be a second walk of the same CONFIG_FILE_NAMES → ~/.yoyo.toml → XDG ladder (two spellings of one policy). So the value is recorded once at load time into a OnceLock with first-writer-wins, matching LOADED_CONFIG_PATH directly above it. Both halves of that rejection are written into src/config.rs and ARCHITECTURE.md rather than left for the next reader to re-derive.

One resolution, both doors. dispatch_tool_with_fallback is the single site both doors funnel through, so effective_sub_agent_model is read there and used for all four things that needed to move together: the primary dispatch, the fallback-distinctness check, the fallback switch note, and — the honesty half — sub_agent_model_label's primary argument, so a failure report names the model the child actually ran on. The label's signature was not widened.

## Verification

• `cargo build, cargo test (17 binaries, 5701 + 12 passed, 0 failed), cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings, cargo fmt -- --check` — all green.
• Tests: parse table, resolution table driving a local OnceLock in both directions (the real global can be set but never unset, so the global could only ever cover one direction per binary), the emission point with a whole-string assert_eq! against the pre-change label, plus the production resolver through its real global for the absent case.
• Module-size register lines re-pasted from what the gate printed.

## Two things worth flagging rather than burying

1. **I invented a provider-prefix routing rule mid-implementation and then deleted it.** My first resolver stripped a `provider/model` prefix and compared it against an env-guessed provider name. It compiled and built fine — but it was untested policy with no fixture, resting on a guess about which provider the session is authenticated for. I replaced it with the minimal contract and stated the real limit in two places: the child inherits the session's provider and key, so a bare model id is required. Refusing to move provider is the safe direction — the alternative sends a provider the parent's token and dies on exactly the 401 the CLAUDE.md workflow trap describes.

2. **The docs claim at the top says the assessment's grep should be re-run before editing — I did, and it held:** sub_agent_model appeared only in sub_agent_model_label. Not a reinvention.

Not done, named rather than implied: no per-dispatch model argument on the sub_agent tool's schema (SubAgentTool::execute is yoagent's), and no cross-provider routing.
